server.add_tool(tool_points_activity_and_outstanding_balance, name='points_activity_and_outstanding_balance', description="Returns aggregate points activity metrics **for a time period** (week, month, quarter, custom range): total points earned, redeemed, expired, and currently outstanding across all customers. Handles comparisons across time periods. This is **data-only reporting about points volume and flow, NOT financial liability valuation**. Used by merchants for operational reporting and understanding program activity levels. This is aggregate analytical data\u2014slight variations acceptable.\n\nDOES NOT ANSWER / BOUNDARIES:\n- **Dollar value of points or financial liability calculation** \u2192 Evidence: Multiple merchants need liability for GAAP/IFRS. For dollar value and accounting liability, see \"Points liability financial report\" (separate entry, may be boundary). This returns points counts only, not dollar conversion.\n- **Points balances for individual customers** \u2192 Use \"Customer points balance lookup\" (Entry #1) or \"Customer list export\" (Entry #6) for individual customer balances. This is aggregate across all customers.\n- **Predictions about future points activity or redemption** \u2192 This is historical/current aggregate. Cannot predict future earning/redemption behavior.\n- **Breakage rate financial implications** \u2192 Breakage rate is data (% of points that expire unused). Financial implications (revenue recognition, deferred liability unwinding) are accounting, not data query.\n- **Accounting treatment or GAAP compliance guidance** \u2192 Financial and accounting advice, out of scope. This gives you points data; your finance team/auditor decides accounting treatment.\n- **Points by earning source** (purchase vs. referral vs. sign-up) \u2192 Use \"Points origin breakdown\" for source-level analysis. Evidence: flaneur.eu - \"Need to differentiate source of earned points.\" This is total activity, not by source.\n- **Why points activity changed** \u2192 This shows the numbers (trend). Causal analysis (why) is prescriptive/analytical beyond data reporting.\n\nPARAMETERS:\n- time_range (required): date range for activity analysis\n- activity_types (optional): filter to specific activities (earned, redeemed, expired, adjusted)\n- breakdown_by (optional): by tier, by earning method (purchase/referral/bonus)\n- comparison_period (optional): compare to previous period, YoY, QoQ\n- include_trend (optional): show trend over time")

