server.add_tool(tool_customer_lifetime_value_ltv, name='customer_lifetime_value_ltv', description="Returns the average total revenue generated per customer since joining the loyalty program **through a specified end date** (realised LTV to date), segmented by membership type (loyalty vs. non-loyalty, by tier, redeemers vs. non-redeemers). Shows long-term value of loyalty members. Used by executives and finance teams to measure program ROI and justify investment. This is aggregate analytical data\u2014slight variations acceptable, not actuarial/predictive LTV modeling.\n\nDOES NOT ANSWER / BOUNDARIES:\n- **Predicted future LTV** (predictive modeling) \u2192 This is historical/realized LTV (what customers have spent so far), not predictive LTV (what they're expected to spend). Predictive LTV requires modeling, churn prediction, cohort assumptions.\n- **Individual customer's LTV** \u2192 Use \"Customer loyalty profile\" (Entry #4) for one customer's total revenue. This is segment-level aggregate.\n- **LTV by cohort** (month/year joined) \u2192 This is overall LTV by segment. Cohort-based LTV analysis would be separate entry (more complex\u2014needs retention curves by cohort).\n- **Margin or profit per customer** \u2192 Revenue only, not costs or profit. Loyalty platform tracks revenue, not full P&L.\n- **Customer acquisition cost (CAC) or CAC:LTV ratio** \u2192 CAC is marketing data, not loyalty data. This shows LTV only.\n- **Churn rate or customer lifetime (tenure)** \u2192 Related metrics but different questions. This shows value (dollars), not retention (time) or churn (loss rate).\n\nPARAMETERS:\n- segment (required): which segment (loyalty members, non-loyalty, tier name, redeemers)\n- calculation_window (optional): limit to customers who joined in specific window (e.g., \"customers who joined in 2024\")\n- comparison_segment (optional): compare to another segment\n- include_tenure (optional): show average days since join (provides LTV time context)\n- include_trend (optional): show LTV trend over time (careful\u2014cohort effects can confuse this)")

